---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { getApp, COMPANY } from '../../data/apps';

const app = getApp('workshop')!;
const updatedDate = 'August 22, 2026';
const sections = [
  { href: '#availability', label: 'Availability' },
  { href: '#identity', label: 'Sign-in and identity' },
  { href: '#sync-offline', label: 'Uploads and offline limits' },
  { href: '#analysis', label: 'URL analysis' },
  { href: '#export-backup', label: 'Export and backup' },
  { href: '#deletion', label: 'Account deletion' },
  { href: '#troubleshooting', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
  { href: '#contact', label: 'Contact' },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  app={app}
  title={`${app.name} Support — ${COMPANY.name}`}
  description="Help with Workshop web and private native builds, Apple and Microsoft sign-in, uploads, URL analysis, export limits, recovery, and account deletion."
  path="/workshop/support"
  page="support"
  heading="Workshop Support"
  documentMeta={`Updated ${updatedDate} · Web current · Native withdrawn for rework`}
  summary="Workshop’s account-backed web workspace remains current. The iPhone and iPad release has been withdrawn while rework continues. There is no public App Store listing, TestFlight acquisition link, public native price, or promised release date."
  sections={sections}
  evidenceNote="Support guidance was reviewed against the current web and native default branches, the matching live web deployment, and the current recovery runbook on August 22, 2026."
  theme="workshop"
>
  <section id="availability" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Current availability</h2>
    <p class="legal-callout">
      The private web workspace is the current Workshop service. The native build
      supports iPhone and iPad on iOS 17 or later, but its public submission is
      withdrawn for rework. Current private access does not imply App Store or
      TestFlight availability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="identity" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Apple and Microsoft sign-in</h2>
    <p>
      Apple and Microsoft create separate Workshop accounts, even when both
      providers use the same email address. If projects appear missing, sign out
      only after confirming the active provider, then return with the provider
      originally used for that workspace.
    </p>
    <p>
      Support can help identify the account path but will not ask for a password,
      verification code, Apple authorization code, Microsoft token, or encrypted
      revocation credential.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sync-offline" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Uploads, sync, and offline limits</h2>
    <p>
      Workshop is online-only for real account data on both web and native.
      Projects, edits, cut lists, materials, shopping, logs, uploads, analysis,
      cross-device changes, and account deletion require a working connection.
      The native app has no local database mirror or offline write queue.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unit-conversion tables are pure client-side calculations and remain usable
      without the server. Native widgets can show a stale last-synced snapshot,
      but that snapshot is not an editable workspace or backup. Do not assume a
      project is safely on the server until its change or upload finishes without
      an error.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a photo or file does not appear, remain signed in to the correct provider,
      reconnect, reopen the project, and retry the upload once. Keep the original
      file until the account-backed copy is visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="analysis" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Project URL analysis</h2>
    <p>
      URL analysis is optional and runs only after you submit a public project
      page. Workshop may reject a private, local, or unsafe network destination. A
      provider outage or missing configuration returns an error rather than a
      fabricated draft.
    </p>
    <p>
      Review the imported parts, dimensions, materials, and instructions before
      saving or building. Workshop and its generated draft do not replace the
      source, safe tool practice, or qualified engineering advice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="export-backup" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Export and backup limits</h2>
    <p>
      The Settings JSON download is a project-list summary. It omits project detail
      tables, account and authentication state, and uploads, and it cannot be
      imported as a restore. Keep original photos, drawings, and any independent
      build records you need.
    </p>
    <p>
      Workshop also maintains checksummed operator recovery bundles. Those backups
      are for service recovery, not a user-downloadable archive. Historical
      bundles can retain deleted data until scheduled expiry; if an older recovery
      point is restored, a human operator must reconcile later deletions before
      reopening the service. This step is required by the runbook and is not
      automated by the restore code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deletion" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Delete a Workshop account</h2>
    <p>
      In the private native build, open <strong>Settings → Account &amp;
      Data</strong>, review the scope, and confirm account deletion. Deletion
      applies only to the active Apple or Microsoft Workshop account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apple-backed deletion revokes stored Apple authorization before Workshop
      removes local account data. If provider revocation fails, Workshop leaves
      the account intact and reports a retryable failure. Microsoft-backed
      deletion removes Workshop data but does not delete or revoke unrelated
      Microsoft account access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Export the project summary first if it is useful, while remembering that it
      omits project details and uploads. For deletion help, email
      <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.privacyEmail}`}>{COMPANY.privacyEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Troubleshooting checklist</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Confirm whether the issue is in the web workspace or private native build.</li>
      <li>Confirm the active Apple or Microsoft provider before changing sessions.</li>
      <li>Reconnect and retry the failed save, upload, or analysis once.</li>
      <li>Keep the original file and avoid deleting the app during a data-recovery issue.</li>
      <li>
        Record the device or browser, Workshop version, project name, time, and
        visible recovery message.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Do not send credentials, private tokens, payment details, or a complete
      project archive unless support asks for a specific redacted sample.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact" class="legal-section">
    <h2>Contact and legal documents</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>{COMPANY.email}</a> for product
      help. Read the <a href="/workshop/privacy">Workshop Privacy Policy</a> for
      storage, providers, backup retention, and deletion boundaries. Use of
      Workshop is subject to the <a href="/terms">Nintek Terms of Use</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
